{
  "fixture": "xx-6-pack-version-pinning-current",
  "description": "A published (id, version) names one bundle for good. The pack is inline and therefore frozen, which is the point: this is where the rule belongs, not on a shipped pack whose numbers have to be free to move. Two versions of `xx-versioned` live side by side, each pinning a different version of the same accounts module. Asking for 2026.1 keeps returning the three-account chart even though 2026.2 exists — an old pin does not drift. Asking for 2026.2 returns the grown one. Asking without a version returns 2026.2, because current means highest version by code point, not whichever file the directory walk happened to reach first; on the first-match rule the answer would differ between two machines. `contentDigest` is derived from the resolution, so it is pinned here where the input is frozen: it is what makes a version that silently changed its content visible, and both implementations must compute it byte-identically. | Successor to `xx-6-pack-version-pinning`, and what moved is the one thing that was never this fixture's subject: it pinned the two contentDigest VALUES literally, so every field the resolved bundle gains — even one that is null for this pack, which declares no such module — changed the hash and turned this red. The digest is DERIVED from the bundle's serialisation, so a literal value welds the engine's internal shape to the contract, the same way a literal formatVersion welded the data format. What this fixture is about is unchanged and now pinned with placeholders: the digest of a pinned version is the SAME one every time it is resolved and the same one a tenant built from that pin reports, while the current version reports a different one. Whether the two differ is already carried by accountCount, which is real data rather than a hash.",
  "covers": [
    "F-PACK-011",
    "NF-2"
  ],
